1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,814
Here's a real-looking report. The evaluation
job in CI, the automated system that

2
00:00:09,854 --> 00:00:13,648
tests every change, fails with
a timeout after 60 seconds.

3
00:00:13,688 --> 00:00:18,605
The commit "Raise eval timeout to 120s"
is on main, the team's main branch.

4
00:00:18,645 --> 00:00:21,579
On the author's laptop,
the same test passes.

5
00:00:21,919 --> 00:00:27,265
You already have a theory. Everybody does.
So make it a quiz with three options.

6
00:00:27,305 --> 00:00:30,699
One: it was never pushed.
Two: CI caches something.

7
00:00:30,739 --> 00:00:34,335
Three: it's on another branch.
Which would you bet on?

8
00:00:34,375 --> 00:00:35,412
Say it out loud.

9
00:00:40,252 --> 00:00:44,627
Hold the theory. People under pressure
skip the one command that would have shown

10
00:00:44,667 --> 00:00:47,843
the cause, because they think
they already know the answer.

11
00:00:47,883 --> 00:00:52,367
The remedy is a ritual: the same commands,
in the same order, every time, including

12
00:00:52,407 --> 00:00:53,675
when you think you know.

13
00:00:55,515 --> 00:00:59,107
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

14
00:00:59,147 --> 00:01:03,572
In the last video you learned the eleven
steps of the root-cause framework.

15
00:01:03,612 --> 00:01:08,394
The third step is "collect evidence", and
it always starts the same way: with the

16
00:01:08,434 --> 00:01:13,335
ten-command diagnosis. git diff is run
twice, once for each of its two comparisons,

17
00:01:13,375 --> 00:01:14,823
so you type eleven lines.

18
00:01:15,163 --> 00:01:19,849
In this video you run those eleven lines
on a repository with a real problem.

19
00:01:19,889 --> 00:01:24,697
For each one I state the question first,
then show the output, then say what it

20
00:01:24,737 --> 00:01:29,545
rules out. We won't solve the problem
today. Today you collect the evidence and

21
00:01:29,585 --> 00:01:33,288
resist the temptation to fix.
The solution is the next video.

22
00:01:33,628 --> 00:01:38,400
The repository is rag-eval, the one you
created in video 3, some days later.

23
00:01:38,440 --> 00:01:43,148
A bare repository on disk, one with no
checked-out files, plays the server.

24
00:01:44,988 --> 00:01:46,566
Here's what you'll walk away with.

25
00:01:46,906 --> 00:01:48,319
After this video you can:

26
00:01:48,659 --> 00:01:51,490
Run the ten-command diagnosis
in order from memory.

27
00:01:51,830 --> 00:01:55,400
Say for each command which question
about the repository it answers.

28
00:01:55,740 --> 00:01:59,716
Explain why the ritual is run
before any state-changing command.

29
00:02:00,056 --> 00:02:04,263
Name the commands of the ritual that can
write to disk, and why that is harmless.

30
00:02:06,103 --> 00:02:10,936
State before hypothesis. The framework
says: understand the state, then form

31
00:02:10,976 --> 00:02:16,194
hypotheses. The ritual is how you get the
state. It's ordered from the outside in.

32
00:02:16,234 --> 00:02:20,746
Where am I? What do my branches follow?
Which other repositories exist?

33
00:02:20,786 --> 00:02:24,658
What does the history look like?
What happened here recently?

34
00:02:24,698 --> 00:02:29,146
What does a name resolve to? What's
in one commit? What's uncommitted?

35
00:02:29,186 --> 00:02:32,544
Which settings are in force?
And what does Git track?

36
00:02:32,884 --> 00:02:34,788
Each command answers one question.

37
00:02:35,128 --> 00:02:39,651
git status: the current branch, its
relation to its upstream as of the last

38
00:02:39,691 --> 00:02:42,997
exchange, and the staged,
unstaged and untracked paths.

39
00:02:43,037 --> 00:02:47,925
Staged means added to the index, and
untracked means the file has no entry there.

40
00:02:48,265 --> 00:02:53,245
git branch -vv: each local branch, the
commit it points at, its upstream, and how

41
00:02:53,285 --> 00:02:54,857
far ahead or behind it is.

42
00:02:55,197 --> 00:02:59,486
git remote -v: the other repositories
this one exchanges data with.

43
00:02:59,826 --> 00:03:06,585
git log --graph --decorate --oneline
--all: the shape of the history, and where

44
00:03:06,625 --> 00:03:08,134
every name points.

45
00:03:08,474 --> 00:03:12,375
git reflog: what happened to HEAD
in this repository, newest first.

46
00:03:12,715 --> 00:03:17,282
git rev-parse: the exact object ID behind
a name, the current branch, and the

47
00:03:17,322 --> 00:03:18,239
repository root.

48
00:03:18,579 --> 00:03:22,553
git show: one commit, with its
author, date, message and change.

49
00:03:22,893 --> 00:03:27,060
git diff: working tree against
index, which is what is not staged.

50
00:03:27,400 --> 00:03:32,210
git diff --cached: index against the last
commit, which is what the next commit

51
00:03:32,250 --> 00:03:33,008
would record.

52
00:03:33,348 --> 00:03:39,122
git config list --show-origin --show-scope:
every setting in effect and the file it

53
00:03:39,162 --> 00:03:39,892
comes from.

54
00:03:40,232 --> 00:03:44,126
git ls-files: the paths in the
index, which are the tracked files.

55
00:03:44,466 --> 00:03:48,815
Why before any state-changing command.
Two reasons, both from the framework.

56
00:03:48,855 --> 00:03:53,261
The evidence you collect describes the
state that produced the symptom, and a

57
00:03:53,301 --> 00:03:56,495
state-changing command replaces
that state with another.

58
00:03:56,535 --> 00:04:00,942
And a fix chosen before the state is
understood is a guess with side effects.

59
00:04:01,282 --> 00:04:06,553
Read-only, with one footnote. All eleven
commands carry the label 🟢 SAFE: they read

60
00:04:06,593 --> 00:04:11,737
state. The footnote is in the textbook's
list of dangerous edge cases: git status

61
00:04:11,777 --> 00:04:17,112
can write. By default it refreshes the cached
file information in the index file and

62
00:04:17,152 --> 00:04:22,167
writes the file back. That never changes
history. It matters in two situations.

63
00:04:22,207 --> 00:04:25,879
When you investigate a damaged
repository, work on a copy.

64
00:04:25,919 --> 00:04:31,062
And in scripts that run in the background,
use git --no-optional-locks status, as

65
00:04:31,102 --> 00:04:32,982
the git-status manual advises.

66
00:04:33,322 --> 00:04:37,464
When the ritual is not enough. The
ten commands describe one clone.

67
00:04:37,504 --> 00:04:42,644
A teammate pushes, and your clone hasn't
heard. So the commands say nothing certain

68
00:04:42,684 --> 00:04:47,700
about the server until you fetch, and
nothing at all about GitHub objects such as

69
00:04:47,740 --> 00:04:52,631
pull requests, rulesets and check results.
origin/main in these outputs is your

70
00:04:52,671 --> 00:04:55,002
cached copy, as of your last exchange.

71
00:04:56,842 --> 00:05:01,501
Think of a pilot's checklist. The pilot
doesn't run it because flying is unfamiliar.

72
00:05:01,541 --> 00:05:06,144
The pilot runs it because the one item you
skip on the day you're in a hurry is the

73
00:05:06,184 --> 00:05:07,095
one that matters.

74
00:05:07,435 --> 00:05:12,199
The analogy breaks in a useful place. A
checklist confirms that things are as

75
00:05:12,239 --> 00:05:16,316
expected. The ritual has no
expectation. You're not ticking boxes.

76
00:05:16,356 --> 00:05:21,245
You're reading output, and every line of
output either supports a hypothesis or

77
00:05:21,285 --> 00:05:24,801
removes one. So each time, say
what the output rules out.

78
00:05:25,141 --> 00:05:29,125
A second way to hold it: each
command reads from a place.

79
00:05:29,165 --> 00:05:33,220
Working tree, index, refs,
reflog, objects, configuration.

80
00:05:33,260 --> 00:05:38,516
When you know which place a command
reads, you know what it can't tell you.

81
00:05:40,356 --> 00:05:44,590
Read the right-hand column from top
to bottom when the table is complete.

82
00:05:44,630 --> 00:05:48,514
Between them, the eleven lines touch
every place in the repository.

83
00:05:48,554 --> 00:05:52,261
That's why the ritual works without
knowing what the problem is.

84
00:05:54,101 --> 00:05:59,045
Keep the symptom on screen: CI times out
at 60 seconds, the fix commit is on main,

85
00:05:59,085 --> 00:06:00,869
the test passes on the laptop.

86
00:06:01,209 --> 00:06:03,637
1. Where am I, and what is uncommitted?

87
00:06:03,977 --> 00:06:09,171
You're on main. It matches origin/main
as of the last exchange with the server.

88
00:06:09,211 --> 00:06:14,206
One tracked file is modified and not
staged, eval/runner.py, and one file is

89
00:06:14,246 --> 00:06:19,771
untracked, run.log. What this rules out:
we're not on some other branch by accident.

90
00:06:19,811 --> 00:06:24,276
If option three was your bet, the
very first command has retired it.

91
00:06:24,616 --> 00:06:27,666
2. Which branches exist,
and what do they follow?

92
00:06:28,006 --> 00:06:32,212
The star marks the current branch.
[origin/main] is its upstream.

93
00:06:32,252 --> 00:06:36,589
No "ahead" or "behind" appears, so
the last commit has been pushed.

94
00:06:36,629 --> 00:06:39,920
feature/cache has no upstream:
it exists only here.

95
00:06:39,960 --> 00:06:45,342
What this rules out, as far as this clone
knows: "it was committed and not pushed".

96
00:06:45,382 --> 00:06:47,040
So option one is gone too.

97
00:06:47,380 --> 00:06:50,644
3. Which other repositories
does this one talk to?

98
00:06:50,984 --> 00:06:55,682
One remote named origin, with the same
address for fetching and pushing.

99
00:06:55,722 --> 00:07:01,210
In your own projects this is a GitHub URL.
What this rules out: a second remote that

100
00:07:01,250 --> 00:07:02,921
CI might be building from.

101
00:07:03,261 --> 00:07:08,091
Try it now, thirty seconds, from memory.
Name the three commands so far, and the

102
00:07:08,131 --> 00:07:11,256
question each one answers.
Say your answer out loud.

103
00:07:16,096 --> 00:07:21,822
git status: where am I, and what's uncommitted?
git branch -vv: what do the branches

104
00:07:21,862 --> 00:07:25,117
follow? git remote -v:
which other repositories?

105
00:07:25,457 --> 00:07:27,645
4. What does the history look like?

106
00:07:27,985 --> 00:07:32,390
Predict: how many commits, and on
how many lines? Say it out loud.

107
00:07:32,430 --> 00:07:33,064
I'll wait.

108
00:07:37,904 --> 00:07:43,864
--all includes every branch, --graph draws
the lines between commits, and --decorate

109
00:07:43,904 --> 00:07:49,007
prints the names that point at each
commit. Three commits, on two lines.

110
00:07:49,347 --> 00:07:54,249
Here's the same history as a picture:
the commits first, then the names.

111
00:07:54,289 --> 00:08:00,014
HEAD, main and origin/main all sit on f29df3b.
feature/cache is one commit, 1c96817,

112
00:08:00,054 --> 00:08:01,112
off to the side.

113
00:08:01,452 --> 00:08:05,310
5. What was done in this
repository, and in which order?

114
00:08:05,650 --> 00:08:09,045
The reflog lists every position
HEAD has had, newest first.

115
00:08:09,085 --> 00:08:13,877
HEAD is the name that says where you are
now. The reflog is a local journal that no

116
00:08:13,917 --> 00:08:18,011
other clone has. The latest action
here was the commit under suspicion.

117
00:08:18,051 --> 00:08:20,223
Nothing after it: no reset, no switch.

118
00:08:20,563 --> 00:08:23,882
6. Which exact object
does a name stand for?

119
00:08:24,222 --> 00:08:29,490
Names can be ambiguous. Object IDs can't.
HEAD and origin/main resolve to the same

120
00:08:29,530 --> 00:08:34,927
ID. --abbrev-ref HEAD prints the current
branch, and --show-toplevel prints the root

121
00:08:34,967 --> 00:08:40,364
of the working tree, which settles whether
you're in the repository you think you're

122
00:08:40,404 --> 00:08:40,558
in.

123
00:08:40,899 --> 00:08:42,877
7. What is in this commit?

124
00:08:43,217 --> 00:08:47,907
Predict before you look: the commit is
called "Raise eval timeout to 120s".

125
00:08:47,947 --> 00:08:49,609
Which files does it change?

126
00:08:54,449 --> 00:08:59,588
The commit changes one file, configs/eval.yaml.
With --stat you get the list of

127
00:08:59,628 --> 00:09:02,931
changed files, and without
it, the full difference.

128
00:09:02,971 --> 00:09:07,520
Keep that line in mind, and don't
act on it yet. I know it's tempting.

129
00:09:07,860 --> 00:09:10,091
8 and 9. What is not committed?

130
00:09:10,430 --> 00:09:13,874
Quick quiz first. Plain git
diff compares which two places?

131
00:09:13,914 --> 00:09:18,479
Option one: the working tree and the
index. Option two: the index and the last

132
00:09:18,519 --> 00:09:19,896
commit. Say it out loud.

133
00:09:24,736 --> 00:09:29,921
Option one. git diff compares the
working tree with the index: a change to

134
00:09:29,961 --> 00:09:34,511
MAX_TIMEOUT_S in eval/runner.py
exists on disk and is not staged.

135
00:09:34,551 --> 00:09:39,947
git diff --cached compares the index
with the last commit and prints nothing:

136
00:09:39,987 --> 00:09:43,689
nothing is staged. --staged
is a synonym of --cached.

137
00:09:44,029 --> 00:09:49,031
Here it is in the three places. The edit
is in the working tree only, and the index

138
00:09:49,071 --> 00:09:52,372
and the last commit still
agree. Silence is output too.

139
00:09:52,412 --> 00:09:55,591
Here it rules out "the change
is staged and waiting".

140
00:09:55,931 --> 00:09:58,194
10. Which settings are in effect?

141
00:09:58,534 --> 00:10:03,146
Identity, the remote's address, and
the two branch.main.* lines that make

142
00:10:03,186 --> 00:10:07,926
origin/main the upstream of main. When
a command behaves differently on two

143
00:10:07,966 --> 00:10:11,304
machines, this listing is
where the difference shows.

144
00:10:11,344 --> 00:10:16,530
And there are the two gc.reflogexpire lines
of the sandbox that video 1 explained.

145
00:10:16,870 --> 00:10:18,818
11. What does Git track?

146
00:10:19,158 --> 00:10:24,515
eval/runner.py is tracked. run.log is
absent, which matches "untracked" in the

147
00:10:24,555 --> 00:10:25,484
status output.

148
00:10:25,824 --> 00:10:29,112
Eleven lines, and nothing in
the repository has changed.

149
00:10:29,152 --> 00:10:33,927
Now, and only now, are you allowed a theory.
You probably have a sharper one than

150
00:10:33,967 --> 00:10:38,801
the bet you made at the start. Write it
down, and write at least two others beside

151
00:10:38,841 --> 00:10:40,584
it. The next video tests them.

152
00:10:42,424 --> 00:10:44,072
Five mistakes to watch for.

153
00:10:44,412 --> 00:10:47,229
Skipping the ritual because
the answer seems known.

154
00:10:47,269 --> 00:10:51,823
Root cause: pressure; the ritual exists
because people under pressure skip the one

155
00:10:51,863 --> 00:10:54,064
command that would have shown the cause.

156
00:10:54,404 --> 00:10:58,759
Reading "up to date with 'origin/main'"
as a statement about the server now.

157
00:10:58,799 --> 00:11:03,617
Root cause: git status compares with a cached
copy, as of the last exchange; the ten

158
00:11:03,657 --> 00:11:07,203
commands say nothing certain
about the server until you fetch.

159
00:11:07,543 --> 00:11:11,268
Running only git diff and
concluding that nothing is pending.

160
00:11:11,308 --> 00:11:16,207
Root cause: git diff has two comparisons;
without --cached it shows working tree

161
00:11:16,247 --> 00:11:19,231
against index, not index
against the last commit.

162
00:11:19,571 --> 00:11:24,535
Trusting a branch name where an ID is needed.
Root cause: names can be ambiguous and

163
00:11:24,575 --> 00:11:29,419
can point at different commits in different
places; git rev-parse gives the object

164
00:11:29,459 --> 00:11:29,597
ID.

165
00:11:29,937 --> 00:11:33,972
Calling the ritual strictly
read-only on a damaged repository.

166
00:11:34,012 --> 00:11:39,296
Root cause: git status refreshes cached
file information in .git/index and writes

167
00:11:39,336 --> 00:11:42,911
the file back; on a damaged
repository, work on a copy.

168
00:11:44,751 --> 00:11:49,405
Now, out of the lab. A data-pipeline
team has a rule for incidents: the first

169
00:11:49,445 --> 00:11:54,099
message in the incident thread is the
pasted output of the ritual, before any

170
00:11:54,139 --> 00:11:59,220
opinion. One week, two engineers disagree
about whether a hotfix reached the server.

171
00:11:59,260 --> 00:12:02,877
One says yes, the other says
the deployment doesn't show it.

172
00:12:02,917 --> 00:12:07,693
The pasted git branch -vv settles the
local half in one line: the branch has an

173
00:12:07,733 --> 00:12:10,741
upstream, and the output
shows whether it's ahead.

174
00:12:11,081 --> 00:12:16,229
The git config list --show-origin --show-scope
output explains why one engineer's

175
00:12:16,269 --> 00:12:21,416
push behaved differently from the other's:
a setting from a file only one of them

176
00:12:21,456 --> 00:12:26,667
had. Neither fact needed a theory. Both
were on the page because the rule put them

177
00:12:26,707 --> 00:12:27,051
there.

178
00:12:27,391 --> 00:12:32,000
And the team knows the limit of the page:
it describes one clone, so the next step

179
00:12:32,040 --> 00:12:36,308
for the server's state is a fetch, and
for the deployment it's a look at the

180
00:12:36,348 --> 00:12:36,818
platform.

181
00:12:38,658 --> 00:12:45,270
Your turn. Lab 35.1, "The ten-command
diagnosis on three repositories", in

182
00:12:45,310 --> 00:12:51,921
lab-manual/m35-diagnosis-method.md is
the full drill, later in the course.

183
00:12:51,961 --> 00:12:58,933
For now, do Exercise 1.3, Level 1, "the
diagnosis ritual on a small state", in

184
00:12:58,973 --> 00:13:05,674
exercises/m01-m05-foundations.md again,
this time from memory, and time it.

185
00:13:06,014 --> 00:13:10,158
Before each command, say out loud which
question it answers and what you expect it

186
00:13:10,198 --> 00:13:14,393
to print for the state you built. The
exercise is done when you can do that without

187
00:13:14,433 --> 00:13:15,413
looking at the card.

188
00:13:17,253 --> 00:13:21,022
Q40: "The course's ten-command diagnosis
ritual is meant to be read-only.

189
00:13:21,062 --> 00:13:24,884
Which of its commands can write to
disk, and why does that matter when you

190
00:13:24,924 --> 00:13:26,658
investigate a damaged repository?"

191
00:13:28,998 --> 00:13:33,775
Answer out loud. A strong answer separates
"does not change history" from "does not

192
00:13:33,815 --> 00:13:38,360
write a byte". It names what is written
and where, says why that is harmless in

193
00:13:38,400 --> 00:13:43,120
ordinary work, and then says what you do
differently when the repository itself is

194
00:13:43,160 --> 00:13:47,995
the evidence. Mention the form of the command
that the manual advises for background

195
00:13:48,035 --> 00:13:48,459
scripts.

196
00:13:50,299 --> 00:13:56,051
Let's land this. You should now be able
to say: before I form a hypothesis, I run

197
00:13:56,091 --> 00:14:01,915
the same eleven lines in the same order.
git status, git branch -vv and git remote

198
00:14:01,955 --> 00:14:07,349
-v tell me where I am and what I am
connected to. git log --graph --decorate

199
00:14:07,389 --> 00:14:13,141
--oneline --all and git reflog tell me the
shape of the history and what happened

200
00:14:13,181 --> 00:14:13,499
here.

201
00:14:13,839 --> 00:14:19,301
git rev-parse and git show tell me exactly
which object a name means and what is in

202
00:14:19,341 --> 00:14:24,207
it. The two git diff commands tell me
what is unstaged and what is staged.

203
00:14:24,247 --> 00:14:29,643
git config list --show-origin --show-scope
and git ls-files tell me which settings

204
00:14:29,683 --> 00:14:31,830
are in force and what Git tracks.

205
00:14:32,170 --> 00:14:37,042
All of this describes one clone, and nothing
certain about the server until I fetch.

206
00:14:38,882 --> 00:14:41,507
Read section 1.11 of Chapter 1.

207
00:14:41,847 --> 00:14:46,353
Write the eleven commands on a card, and
run them on three repositories of your own

208
00:14:46,393 --> 00:14:47,776
without changing anything.

209
00:14:48,116 --> 00:14:54,704
Challenge: Exercise 1.9, Level 4, "git
log says there are no commits", in

210
00:14:54,744 --> 00:14:57,700
exercises/m01-m05-foundations.md.

211
00:14:58,040 --> 00:15:01,488
Eleven lines, and a real
repository read from end to end.

212
00:15:01,528 --> 00:15:06,567
Put them on a card before the next video.
Next time, a worked example: the fix that

213
